-- title:
--   Cartier quadruple and Deligne lines at a point with y(ξ)≠ 0
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $p$, an algebraically closed field $k$ of characteristic $p$, and a ring homomorphism $\iota\colon W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})\to W(k)$. Write $\bar{R}=W(k)/pW(k)$ (the quotient by the ideal generated by $p$) and $\bar\iota$ for the composite of $\iota$ with the quotient map; $E_1=\mathtt{edgeRingCharP}\,p\,\bar R$ denotes the edge chart ring over $\bar R$, with its distinguished elements $\xi,\eta$, and $\psi_{E}$ the composite of the quotient map $W(k)\to\bar R$ with the structural algebra map $\bar R\to E_1$.
--
--   The data on the source side consist of a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $\Phi$ over $\bar R$ (an object of `FormalODModule p` $\bar R$: a commutative two-dimensional formal group law with an action of $W(\mathbb{F}_{p^2})$ and an endomorphism $\varpi$ with $\varpi\circ\varpi=[p]$ and $\varpi\circ a=\sigma(a)\circ\varpi$), subject to: `hΦ`, that $\Phi$ is special for $\bar\iota$, i.e. the submodules $\mathrm{Lie}^0$ and $\mathrm{Lie}^1$ of the Lie module are complementary and each invertible; `hΦ4`, that the action of $p$ on $\Phi$ has kernel of degree $p^4$; `h0Φ`, that $\mathrm{Lie}^0$ is contained in the kernel of the linear part `lieVarpi` of $\varpi$; `hcΦ`, that the two graded pieces of degree $0$ and $1$ of the Cartier module of $\Phi$ (the subgroups on which the Teichmüller lifts of $\mathbb{F}_{p^2}$ act through $\bar\iota(\tau(c))^{p^n}$) are complementary additive subgroups. Attached to `hcΦ` is the graded Cartier module datum `Φ.toGradedCartierModuleData`, and $r_\Phi$ is an additive map from $\mathbb{Z}_p^{2}$ to its $N$-module, normalised by `hrΦ`: for every additive $L$ from the Cartier module to the $N$-module which is a canonical $L$-map, $r_\Phi$ maps $\mathbb{Z}_p^{2}$ bijectively onto the degree-$0$ eta piece $\mathtt{etaPiece}\,L\,0$.
--
--   Further, $g\in\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb{Q}_p)$ with `hg`: the matrix of $g$ is $\mathrm{diag}(p,1)$.
--
--   The edge family is a formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module $X$ over $E_1$ together with $\gamma_0,\gamma_1$ in its Cartier module, subject to: `hγ`, that $\gamma$ is a homogeneous $V$-basis for the structure map $\psi_E\circ\iota$, i.e. $\gamma_i$ lies in the graded piece of degree $i$ and the matrix of tangents $(\mathrm{tangent}(\gamma_i)_k)$ has unit determinant; `hγa`, that $\gamma$ has the edge structure constants $\mathtt{edgeRingConstants}$ built from $\xi,\eta$, i.e. for each $i$ and each $N$ the element $\varpi(\gamma_i)$ agrees, modulo $V^N$ of the Cartier module, with $\sum_{m<N}V^{m}\bigl(a_{m,i}\cdot\gamma_{\pi(m,i)}\bigr)$; `hXs`, that $X$ is special for $\psi_E\circ\iota$; and `hX4`, that $X$ has height $4$.
--
--   The node point is a ring homomorphism $f_0\colon E_1\to\bar R$ with `hf₀`: $f_0$ is a retraction of the structural map $\bar R\to E_1$; `hf₀ξ`: $f_0(\xi)=0$; and `hf₀η`: $f_0(\eta)=0$. Over the node there are $m\in\mathbb{N}$ and a two-variable power series datum $\rho_0$ over $\bar R$ with `hρ₀`: $\rho_0$ is an isogeny $\Phi\to X\otimes_{f_0}\bar R$ of height $4m$, that is, an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism whose kernel has degree $p^{4m}$. Writing $t_0$ for the rigidified object $(X\otimes_{f_0}\bar R,\;m,\;\rho_0\bmod p)$ over $\bar R$, the hypotheses `hOD₀`, `hcb` and `hcΦg` state respectively that the reduction $\rho$ of $t_0$ is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism from $\bar\Phi$ to $\bar X$, that the degree-$0$ and degree-$1$ graded pieces of $\mathtt{XbarS}\,t_0\,\mathrm{id}$ are complementary, and that the same holds for $\mathtt{PhibarS}$; these make the rigid numbering map $\mathtt{rigidNum}$ from $\mathbb{Z}_p^{2}$ to the $N$-module of the graded datum of $\mathtt{XbarS}\,t_0\,\mathrm{id}$ available. The normalisation hypothesis `hN` fixes $a\in\mathbb{N}$ and requires, for each $i\in\{0,1\}$, that $p^{a}$ times the value of $\mathtt{rigidNum}$ at the $i$-th standard basis vector of $\mathbb{Z}_p^{2}$ equals $p^{a+m}$ times the $i$-th entry of the pair $\bigl(\mathtt{nMk}(\bar\gamma_0,0),\;\mathtt{nVarpi}(\mathtt{nMk}(\bar\gamma_1,0))\bigr)$, where $\bar\gamma_i$ denotes the image of $\gamma_i$ under the successive base changes along $f_0$, along the quotient by the ideal generated by $p$, and along the reduction map of the identity.
--
--   The edge isogeny is a homomorphism $\rho_1$ of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules from $(X\otimes_{f_0}\bar R)\otimes_{\bar R}E_1$ to $X$, with the two explicit Cartier-module identities `hρ₁0` and `hρ₁1`: the map induced by $\rho_1$ on Cartier modules sends the base change of $\gamma_0$ to $p\cdot\gamma_0-V\bigl(\tau(\eta^{p^{2}})\cdot\gamma_1\bigr)$ and the base change of $\gamma_1$ to $p\cdot\gamma_1-V\bigl(\tau(\xi^{p^{2}})\cdot\gamma_0\bigr)$, where $\tau$ is the Teichmüller lift and $V$ is the integral Verschiebung; and `hρ₁h`: the series of $\rho_1$ is an isogeny of height $4$.
--
--   Finally, $\Omega$ is an algebraically closed field which is a $\mathbb{Z}_p$-algebra, and $y\colon E_1\to\Omega$ a ring homomorphism with `hyξ`: $y(\xi)\neq 0$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exist a Drinfeld datum $Q$ over $\Omega$ for the base $(\mathbb{Z}_p,\mathbb{Q}_p)$ and uniformiser $p$ (a pair of full lattices $N_0\subseteq N_1$ in $\mathbb{Q}_p^{2}$ at each point of $\mathrm{Spec}\,\Omega$ with $pN_1\subseteq N_0$ and open membership loci, invertible modules $T_0,T_1$ with maps $\Pi_0,\Pi_1$ composing to multiplication by $p$, and maps $u_0,u_1$ from the lattice base changes to the stalks satisfying the listed compatibilities) and a Deligne datum $d$ over $\Omega$ (an assignment to each full lattice $M$ of a submodule $\mathrm{line}(M)$ of the base change of $M$ with invertible quotient, monotone under inclusions, equivariant for homotheties, and non-degenerate at every prime) such that the following four statements hold.
--
--   First, the base change along $y$ of the rigidified object $\bigl(X,\;m+1,\;(\rho_1\bmod p)\circ(\rho_0\text{ transported to }E_1\bmod p)\bigr)$ satisfies `IsCartierQuadruple` relative to $\iota$, `hcΦ`, $r_\Phi$, the structure map $y\circ\psi_E$ and $Q$: its reduction is an $\mathcal{O}_D$-homomorphism from the reduction of $\Phi$ to that of the base-changed $X$, and there are $\Omega$-linear identifications of $Q.T_0$ and $Q.T_1$ with the zero and one Lie pieces of the base-changed $X$ carrying $\Pi_0$ and $\Pi_1$ to `lieVarpi`, together with, at each point of $\mathrm{Spec}\,\Omega$, the clauses characterising membership in $N_0$ and in $N_1$ by the existence of eta-sections of the corresponding graded Cartier module data over a localisation, and the matching clauses tying $u_0$ and $u_1$ to those sections.
--
--   Second, $Q.\mathtt{IsQuadrupleOf}\,d$: at every point $x$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,\Omega$ the edge non-degeneracy condition holds for the pair of lattices of $Q$ at $x$, and the kernels of $u_0$ at $x$ and of $u_1$ at $x$ are the lines assigned by the base change of $d$ to the local ring to these two lattices.
--
--   Third, $d.\mathrm{line}$ of the standard full lattice $\mathbb{Z}_p^{2}$ is the $\Omega$-span of the single element $y(\xi)\otimes e_0+1\otimes e_1$.
--
--   Fourth, $d.\mathrm{line}$ of the translate of the standard full lattice by $g$ is the image, under the base-change isomorphism $\mathtt{actBaseChange}\,\Omega\,g$ associated with $g$ and the standard lattice, of the $\Omega$-span of $1\otimes e_0+y(\eta)\otimes e_1$.
--
--   This is the geometric-fibre computation for the rigidified edge family in the Čerednik–Drinfeld uniformisation: at a point of the edge chart lying on the branch where $\xi$ is invertible, the Cartier quadruple attached to the rigidified special formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-module is realised by an explicit Deligne datum on the two vertices $\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ and $\mathrm{diag}(p,1)\mathbb{Z}_p^2$ of the Bruhat–Tits tree. It is used by [`CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.forall_isCartierQuadruple_map_line_eq_of_rigidNum_single_eq_of_edge_isogeny`](thm.html#CerednikDrinfeld.FormalODModule.forall_isCartierQuadruple_map_line_eq_of_rigidNum_single_eq_of_edge_isogeny), which assembles the three cases (the two branches and the node) over all points of the edge chart.
